  // Apply fn() only to text nodes in an HTML string (skip content inside tags)
  

  // ── Term definitions (Article 3 tooltips) ────────────────────────────────
  // Build Article 3 term tooltip list from blocks (any language).
  // Matches both EN-style ('term' means ...) and continental-style («term»: ... / „term" ...).
  // Only multi-word terms are included — single common words (risk, data…) are too noisy.
  // Article 3 defines the Act's terms, and each definition becomes a tooltip.
  // Every language states them differently, so three shapes are recognised:
  //   quoted    EN "'AI system' means ...", DE '„KI-System“ ein ...',
  //             DA '»AI-system«: ...', FI '”term” ...' (same mark both ends)
  //   prefixed  GA 'ciallaíonn ‘córas intleachta saorga’ ...' — verb first
  //   bare      SV 'AI-system: ...', LT 'DI sistema – ...' — no quotes at all
  // Before this, DA/FI/GA/LT/SV matched nothing and contributed no tooltips.
  

  // ── Context tooltips ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Load context_tooltips.tsv once. Wide format — one row per concept, one column per
  // language. Each language cell holds "trigger phrase||summary text".
  // Lines starting with # are comments and are ignored.
  

  // Build ctxTips for the active language from the already-loaded allCtxRows.
  // Called after every language switch.
  

  // ── Tooltip applier ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Apply term and context tooltips to an HTML string, touching only text nodes.
  // ctx tips run first so they take precedence over Art 3 auto-terms for overlapping phrases.
  

  // ── Ordinals (PT / IT / ES) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Portuguese, Italian, and Spanish use ordinal suffixes (1.º, n.ºs, etc.)
  // that look like period-space in the raw JSON source. stripOrdinals() removes
  // them before regex matching; applyOrdinals() re-inserts them after rendering.
    function stripOrdinals(s) { return window.__aiactUtils.stripOrdinals(s, currentLang); }

  

  // ── Text rendering ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // renderText() is the core text pipeline used by all render functions.
  // Pipeline: stash links → HTML-escape → restore links (detect AI Act xrefs)
  //           → footnote superscripts → link patterns → term tooltips → ordinals
  // Depends on: fnMap, fnRefCounter, linkPatterns, termDefs, ctxTips, renderContext
    function renderText(raw) {
    return window.__renderPipeline.renderText(raw, RS);
  }

  // ── Cross-reference link patterns ────────────────────────────────────────
  // Build cross-reference link patterns from the current titles data.
  // Each language uses its own localized word for "Article" / "Annex" / "Recital",
  // including the plural form so "Artikelen 5 en 6" etc. are also linked.
  //
  // External-reference detection:
  //   • Number out of range (AI Act has Articles 1–113) → skip
  //   • Followed by TEU/TFEU/EEA abbreviation → skip
  //   • EU regulation citation pattern "(EU/UE/EG) YYYY/N" → skip
  //   • EN: "of Regulation …", "of Directive …", "of the Treaty …" → skip
  //
  // Uses Unicode-aware lookbehind instead of \b so words starting with
  // non-ASCII characters (Czech Č, Greek Ά, Bulgarian Ч …) are matched.
  // opts.selfItemRefs — when false, "Article 6" / "Annex III" are left as plain
  // text instead of being linked to this document's own provisions. Amending
  // regulations need this: inside the Digital Omnibus, "Article 6" means Article 6
  // of the act being amended (the AI Act in its Article 1, Regulations 2018/1139
  // and 2023/1230 in its Articles 2 and 3), never one of its own four articles.
  // Recital references are still linked — those are the document's own.
  


